Chapter: BrotliInterop
  cites Compress chapter Brotli

 A probe for build/brotli-interop-test.ps1. It prints each input and its
 brotli-compressed bytes as decimals so a real RFC 7932 decoder (.NET's
 BrotliStream on the host) can prove the output is genuinely Brotli, not merely
 decodable by our own decoder. A round-trip through our own pair could not see
 that this chapter emitted a private byte-aligned format for months. This has no
 .expected: the host harness is the judge, so the file carries a .skip.

 We say:

Section: Body

  run-of : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  run-of (v) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else run-of v n (i + 1) (list-push acc v)

  cyc-byte : Integer -> Integer
  cyc-byte (i) = list-at [65, 66, 65, 67, 65, 66, 68, 65] (i - (i / 8) * 8)

  gen-cyc : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gen-cyc (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else gen-cyc n (i + 1) (list-push acc (cyc-byte i))

 Bytes above 128, where a chapter that only understood ASCII would show it.

  hi-byte : Integer -> Integer
  hi-byte (i) = list-at [200, 213, 213, 249, 213, 137, 213, 255, 213, 180, 213, 129] (i - (i / 12) * 12)

  gen-hi : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gen-hi (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else gen-hi n (i + 1) (list-push acc (hi-byte i))

  rnd-byte : Integer -> Integer
  rnd-byte (i) = (i * 97 + (i / 7) * 31 + 11) - ((i * 97 + (i / 7) * 31 + 11) / 256) * 256

  gen-rnd : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gen-rnd (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else gen-rnd n (i + 1) (list-push acc (rnd-byte i))

 Built to reach the insert-and-copy groups the smaller cases cannot. The group a
 command lands in depends on BOTH its insert code and its copy code, and the nine
 group bases are irregular -- 448 sits where the table's shape suggests 512. Only
 a long literal stretch followed by a long copy reaches the far groups: an insert
 of 250 is insert code 17 and a copy of 100 is copy code 16, which is group base
 640, and nothing else in this probe goes near it. A wrong base there is a stream
 the oracle rejects with no other symptom.

  bg-lcg : Integer -> Integer
  bg-lcg (s) = bit-and (s * 1103515245 + 12345) 2147483647

  gen-junk : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gen-junk (n) (i) (s) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let s2 = bg-lcg s
    in gen-junk n (i + 1) s2 (list-push acc ((s2 / 65536) - ((s2 / 65536) / 256) * 256))

  gen-pat : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gen-pat (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else gen-pat n (i + 1) (list-push acc (33 + (i * 7 - ((i * 7) / 89) * 89)))

  bt-word : Integer -> List Integer
  bt-word (k) =
    if k == 0 then [116, 104, 101]
    else if k == 1 then [113, 117, 105, 99, 107]
    else if k == 2 then [98, 114, 111, 119, 110]
    else if k == 3 then [102, 111, 120]
    else if k == 4 then [106, 117, 109, 112, 115]
    else if k == 5 then [111, 118, 101, 114]
    else if k == 6 then [108, 97, 122, 121]
    else if k == 7 then [100, 111, 103]
    else if k == 8 then [97, 110, 100]
    else if k == 9 then [116, 104, 101, 110]
    else if k == 10 then [114, 117, 110, 115]
    else if k == 11 then [102, 97, 114]
    else if k == 12 then [97, 119, 97, 121]
    else if k == 13 then [105, 110, 116, 111]
    else if k == 14 then [116, 104, 101]
    else [119, 111, 111, 100, 115]

  gen-text : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gen-text (n) (s) (acc) =
    if list-length acc >= n then acc
    else let s2 = bg-lcg s
    in let k = (s2 / 4096) - ((s2 / 4096) / 16) * 16
    in let sep = if (s2 / 256) - ((s2 / 256) / 16) * 16 == 0 then [46, 32] else [32]
    in gen-text n s2 (acc & bt-word k & sep)

 Real multibyte UTF-8 prose, which is the ONE shape context mode 2 is built for
 and the one this probe did not have. Mode 2 keys a literal's context on the
 character CLASS of the two previous bytes, and its tables give UTF-8 lead bytes
 and continuation bytes classes of their own. Every other case here is ASCII or
 arbitrary high bytes, where that classification says nothing the low six bits of
 the previous byte do not already say, so mode 2 could never win and the encoder
 could not be measured against it honestly.

  u8-word : Integer -> List Integer
  u8-word (k) =
    if k == 0 then [208, 191, 209, 128, 208, 184, 208, 178, 208, 181, 209, 130]
    else if k == 1 then [208, 188, 208, 184, 209, 128]
    else if k == 2 then [208, 177, 209, 139, 209, 129, 209, 130, 209, 128, 208, 176, 209, 143]
    else if k == 3 then [208, 186, 208, 190, 209, 128, 208, 184, 209, 135, 208, 189, 208, 181, 208, 178, 208, 176, 209, 143]
    else if k == 4 then [208, 187, 208, 184, 209, 129, 208, 176]
    else if k == 5 then [208, 191, 209, 128, 209, 139, 208, 179, 208, 176, 208, 181, 209, 130]
    else if k == 6 then [209, 135, 208, 181, 209, 128, 208, 181, 208, 183]
    else if k == 7 then [208, 187, 208, 181, 208, 189, 208, 184, 208, 178, 209, 131, 209, 142]
    else if k == 8 then [209, 129, 208, 190, 208, 177, 208, 176, 208, 186, 209, 131]
    else if k == 9 then [208, 184]
    else if k == 10 then [208, 183, 208, 176, 209, 130, 208, 181, 208, 188]
    else if k == 11 then [230, 151, 165, 230, 156, 172, 232, 170, 158]
    else if k == 12 then [227, 131, 134, 227, 130, 173, 227, 130, 185, 227, 131, 136]
    else if k == 13 then [227, 129, 167, 227, 129, 153]
    else if k == 14 then [116, 114, 195, 168, 115]
    else [110, 97, 195, 175, 118, 101]

  gen-utf8 : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  gen-utf8 (n) (s) (acc) =
    if list-length acc >= n then acc
    else let s2 = bg-lcg s
    in let k = (s2 / 4096) - ((s2 / 4096) / 16) * 16
    in let sep = if (s2 / 256) - ((s2 / 256) / 16) * 16 == 0 then [46, 32] else [32]
    in gen-utf8 n s2 (acc & u8-word k & sep)

 Longer than one meta-block, with a repeat that reaches ACROSS the cut. A region
 is 65536 bytes, so the tail here sits in the second meta-block and matches
 material from the first, at a distance inside the matcher's window but far
 outside the region it lives in. A matcher started fresh at each cut cannot find
 it at any window size, so this is the case that tells a real window from a
 declared one. It is also the only case that runs the carried previous byte
 between regions, which context modelling needs and which no shorter input
 reaches.

 Built with list-push rather than append: at eighty thousand bytes an accumulator
 copied per byte is quadratic and does not finish.

 THE ORIGINAL IS NOT PRINTED. `show-bytes` builds its line by appending to a Text
 once per byte, which is quadratic in the same way -- at eight thousand bytes that
 is tolerable and at seventy-eight thousand it is billions of character copies,
 and the probe died before reaching this case. The harness regenerates these bytes
 from the formula below instead, which is a better check than shipping them: the
 comparison is then against an independent implementation rather than against our
 own output.

 Ninety-six DISTINCT words from RFC 7932's own corpus, one after another with
 single spaces. Every word appears exactly once, so the matcher has almost
 nothing to find and only the dictionary can shrink this. That is deliberate:
 the other cases are built so a feature CAN help, and this one is built so
 nothing else can.

